<DocsLayout
  title="Modelling & estimation"
  description="A precise guide to the modelling paths, transformer integrations, uncertainty evidence, and parameter-estimation limits in super-charge-ml."
  current="modeling"
>
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Models, uncertainty, and limits</p>
  <h1>Choose the model<br /><em>after the evidence.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">super-charge-ml can search and evaluate several model families, including an optional FT-Transformer path, and can pretrain a bounded tabular JEPA representation. It does not pretend that either adapter is a complete multimodal modelling product.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Read this page before installing extras.</strong> The supported beta is still the narrow tabular library workflow. Deep-learning and text-embedding integrations are available as explicit optional paths with separate evidence. Tabular JEPA is experimental; multimodal JEPA is not implemented. Parameter estimation is a separate scientific evidence track that can be attached to an <code>MLProject</code> run, but it never changes model selection.</p></div>

  <h2>The decision path</h2>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <figcaption class="diagram-caption"><span>MODEL SELECTION</span><strong>A larger model never repairs an untrusted dataset.</strong></figcaption>
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-node"><span>01</span><b>Understand</b><small>identity · labels · leakage</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>02</span><b>Screen</b><small>baselines · constraints</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>03</span><b>Compare</b><small>CV · calibration · cost</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>04</span><b>Explain</b><small>limits · sensitivity · review</small></div>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-rail"><span>release evidence</span><i></i><span>package · infer · monitor</span></div>
  </figure>
  <p>The planner owns the comparison: it screens model families against task, data shape, resource limits, and declared policy. A candidate is not a release because it has the highest point score. The run must retain the split identity, trial parameters, metrics, uncertainty, calibration diagnostics, explanation evidence, package verification, and known limitations.</p>

  <h2>What modelling exists today</h2>
  <div class="model-table" role="region" aria-label="Modeling support matrix" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>How to use it</th><th>Current claim</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tabular beta</th><td>Use <code>MLProject</code> with a declared target and tabular input. The planner can compare the built-in linear, tree, and other candidate families under the supported workflow.</td><td><span class="status status-supported">Supported*</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">FT-Transformer</th><td>Install <code>super-charge-ml[deeplearning]</code>. The optional <code>torch_ft_transformer</code> adapter uses attention over feature tokens and participates in the model-family search when its dependency and resource checks pass.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Other PyTorch families</th><td>The same optional extra exposes MLP, residual, TabM-style ensemble, and RealMLP-style families. They are explicit candidates, not silent dependencies.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Text embeddings</th><td>Install <code>super-charge-ml[embeddings]</code> for the sentence-transformers integration. The text transformer runs in the isolated worker; if unavailable, the documented fallback is TF-IDF.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">TabPFN</th><td>The explicit adapter remains extension-only until the external package, model-weight license, token custody, and independent evidence are supplied.</td><td><span class="status status-extension">Extension-only</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tabular JEPA</th><td><code>JepaEstimator</code> learns numeric-tabular representations with masked context prediction, an EMA target encoder, variance regularization, worker isolation, checkpointable weights, and <code>transform</code> output. It is not a multimodal JEPA foundation model.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Parameter estimation</th><td><code>estimate_parameters</code> supports declared linear, logistic, Bayesian Gaussian, IPW ATE, and grouped random-intercept estimators, with explicit assumptions, uncertainty, sensitivity, and independent reference evidence. The project API persists the result as <code>parameter_estimation.json</code>.</td><td><span class="status status-experimental">Bounded API</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">* “Supported” is intentionally narrow: it describes the explicitly gated tabular library path, not every dataset, model, accelerator, or deployment environment.</p>

  <h2>Try an optional transformer path</h2>
  <p>The extra is a deliberate boundary. It keeps the base install small and makes the dependency, worker process, resource cost, and failure mode visible in the run. Start with a small budget and compare the transformer against a simple baseline on the same split.</p>
  <pre><code>{`# Optional PyTorch/Keras model families, including FT-Transformer
pip install "super-charge-ml[deeplearning]"

# Optional sentence-transformer text encoding
pip install "super-charge-ml[embeddings]"`}</code></pre>
  <p>Before treating the result as useful, inspect the run evidence for the resolved adapter, dependency versions, seed, train/validation/test identity, resource observations, failed trials, and explanation limitations. A successful import only proves that the code can start; it does not prove that the model learned the intended signal.</p>

  <h2>Pretrain a tabular JEPA representation</h2>
  <p>JEPA is a transformer-adjacent self-supervised representation workflow, not a target-label model. The current implementation accepts a finite numeric matrix, masks feature values, predicts an EMA target representation, and exposes embeddings for a downstream model you choose. The downstream benchmark, split, and model card remain your responsibility.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml.models.deeplearning import JepaEstimator

encoder = JepaEstimator(
    embedding_size=64,
    mask_probability=0.3,
    epochs=50,
    random_state=42,
)
embeddings = encoder.fit_transform(numeric_features)

# Evaluate the representation with a declared downstream split.
downstream_model.fit(embeddings[train], labels[train])
score = downstream_model.score(embeddings[test], labels[test])`}</code></pre>
  <p>This is experimental. It does not decode raw documents, images, audio, video, graphs, or geospatial objects; it does not provide a universal pretrained checkpoint; and it is not automatically included in supervised model search.</p>

  <h2>JEPA is implemented narrowly, not implied broadly</h2>
  <p>The repository now has an explicit tabular JEPA path. It uses numeric feature masking, a context encoder, an EMA target encoder, an embedding-space prediction objective, and variance regularization to make collapse observable. It runs in the isolated PyTorch worker and returns a representation through <code>transform</code>.</p>
  <div class="split-cards">
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Today</span><h3>What is honest</h3><p>Tabular numeric JEPA is an experimental representation trainer. It has a defined objective, serialized worker state, deterministic seed, and a contract test. It is not registered as a supervised model-search candidate.</p><p class="note">The representation still needs downstream transfer benchmarks and independent domain review.</p></div>
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Still separate</span><h3>What is not included</h3><p>There is no document, image, audio, video, graph, or geospatial JEPA encoder; no universal pretrained checkpoint; and no claim that the embedding is semantically meaningful without a domain benchmark.</p><p class="note">A new modality needs its own encoder, masking policy, transfer test, and explanation boundary.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Parameter estimation is not hyperparameter tuning</h2>
  <p>This distinction matters. The current project has useful pieces, but they answer different questions:</p>
  <div class="split-cards">
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Implemented</span><h3>Choosing a model</h3><p>Hyperparameter search explores declared spaces. Bayesian model comparison evaluates repeated score differences. Calibration and conformal methods quantify predictive behaviour. Cross-validation can report intervals around metrics.</p></div>
    <div class="mini-card"><span class="mini-label">Implemented, bounded</span><h3>Estimating a parameter</h3><p><code>EstimandSpec</code>, <code>EstimationPolicy</code>, and <code>EstimationResult</code> record the parameter definition, likelihood, assumptions, covariance or posterior uncertainty, intervals, sensitivity evidence, and reference calculation. Causal and hierarchical paths require explicit assumptions and report their unproven boundaries.</p></div>
  </div>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <figcaption class="diagram-caption"><span>THREE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS</span><strong>Do not read one as another.</strong></figcaption>
    <div class="planes">
      <div class="plane"><span>Predictive uncertainty</span><strong>What might this model predict for a new case?</strong><p>Conformal intervals, prediction sets, calibration diagnostics.</p></div>
      <div class="plane plane-execution"><span>Model selection uncertainty</span><strong>How stable is the comparison between candidates?</strong><p>Repeated folds, seed intervals, Bayesian score comparisons.</p></div>
      <div class="plane plane-evidence"><span>Statistical parameter uncertainty</span><strong>What is the uncertainty around a defined population quantity?</strong><p>Estimand, identification, estimator, covariance, standard errors, sensitivity.</p></div>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <h2>Run a declared estimate</h2>
  <p>The estimation API is intentionally explicit. You supply the design matrix, outcome, scientific declaration, and covariance policy; the result tells you what was estimated, how many rows were used, how uncertainty was computed, and whether the independent reference calculation agreed.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scml import EstimandSpec, EstimationPolicy, estimate_parameters

estimand = EstimandSpec(
    name="conversion_log_odds",
    outcome="converted",
    population="eligible visitors in the 2026 observation window",
    likelihood="bernoulli",
    feature_names=("treatment", "prior_visits"),
    assumptions=("rows are conditionally independent",),
)
report = estimate_parameters(
    features,
    converted,
    estimand,
    EstimationPolicy(estimator="logistic", covariance="hc3"),
)
report.write("artifacts/parameter-estimation.json")`}</code></pre>
  <p>That report is not automatically a causal conclusion. It is a reproducible estimate under the assumptions you declared. For a treatment effect, use <code>ipw_ate</code> or <code>aipw_ate</code> with explicit consistency, conditional exchangeability, and positivity assumptions. For grouped observations, use <code>hierarchical_linear</code> with explicit random-intercept assumptions and cluster labels. These methods report what they did and what they did not prove; an independent domain review is still required.</p>

  <h2>Choose the scientific estimator deliberately</h2>
  <div class="model-table" role="region" aria-label="Parameter estimation methods" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Estimator</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Evidence and limits</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>linear</code> / <code>logistic</code></th><td>Declared Gaussian or Bernoulli associations.</td><td>IID, HC3, or clustered covariance; intervals, missingness and shift diagnostics, optional bootstrap sensitivity, and an independent reference calculation.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>bayesian_linear</code></th><td>A Gaussian design with a declared conjugate normal-inverse-gamma prior.</td><td>Posterior covariance, credible intervals, prior-strength sensitivity, and an independent positive-definite solve. Robust, clustered, Bayesian logistic, and Bayesian bootstrap variants fail closed.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>ipw_ate</code></th><td>A binary-treatment average treatment effect under a declared propensity model.</td><td>Hájek IPW, clipping and overlap diagnostics, effective sample sizes, influence-function variance, and an independent propensity reference. This does not prove exchangeability, positivity, or absence of unmeasured confounding.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>aipw_ate</code></th><td>A binary-treatment average treatment effect with propensity and outcome nuisance models.</td><td>Augmented IPW score, clipping and overlap diagnostics, influence-function variance, fitted-nuisance limitations, and an independent propensity-plus-outcome reference. Cross-fitting and nuisance-model uncertainty propagation are not implemented; this remains a bounded scientific estimator.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>hierarchical_linear</code></th><td>Grouped Gaussian outcomes with a random-intercept assumption.</td><td>Choose the bounded <code>moment_gls</code> default or explicit <code>reml</code> variance-component estimation, then inspect GLS coefficients, cluster evidence, optimizer/reference diagnostics, and stated uncertainty limits. Variance-component uncertainty, random slopes, nested/crossed effects, and fully Bayesian multilevel inference remain out of scope.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">The estimator is part of the claim. Do not label a model-selection coefficient as a scientific parameter estimate. For <code>aipw_ate</code>, opt into <code>cross_fitting_folds</code> when held-out nuisance scoring is required; the report still identifies what uncertainty has not been propagated.</p>

  <h2>What you can inspect now</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Search decisions:</strong> the plan records the candidate family, parameter space, budget, pruning decisions, and failed or deferred trials.</li>
    <li><strong>Predictive evidence:</strong> classification and regression runs can retain calibration diagnostics and conformal prediction intervals when the policy and data volume allow them.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparison evidence:</strong> repeated evaluation can report score intervals and a decision instead of pretending a single split is certainty.</li>
    <li><strong>Explanation evidence:</strong> linear contributions, permutation-style reports, and bounded perturbation checks are recorded with method parameters and known representation limits.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>These artifacts help you decide whether a model is useful. They do not turn a predictive score into a causal estimate, and they do not supply a treatment effect, coefficient standard error, Bayesian posterior, or identified population parameter.</p>

  <h2>Parameter-estimation acceptance bar</h2>
  <p>To promote this capability into a release workflow, the API must answer these questions without reconstructing intent from logs:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What exact estimand is being estimated, for which population and observation window?</li>
    <li>What assumptions identify it, and which assumptions are testable versus declared?</li>
    <li>Which estimator and optimization procedure ran, with the resolved parameters and convergence diagnostics?</li>
    <li>How was uncertainty estimated: robust covariance, bootstrap, profile likelihood, or posterior—and why?</li>
    <li>Can another evaluator reproduce the estimate from raw snapshot, split, seed, software lock, and artifact digest?</li>
    <li>What sensitivity, subgroup, missing-data, and leakage checks would change the interpretation?</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Current answer:</strong> predictive modelling and a bounded estimation evidence track are real and can be persisted from <code>MLProject</code>. The estimator contracts cover transparent Gaussian/Bernoulli, conjugate Bayesian, IPW ATE, and grouped random-intercept paths; stronger causal identification, advanced Bayesian models, and modality-specific estimators remain separate work.</p></div>

